<h1>Senior Project Architecture & System Evolution</h1>
<div class="header-sub">
    <strong>Project Title:</strong> Code Summarization & Green Computing Using AST, PDG & Agentic AI<br>
    <strong>Candidate Name:</strong> K. Leela Krishna (24CSB0A30)
</div>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Document Objective:</strong> This document provides a complete humanized overview of the project's evolution. It contrasts the <strong>Existing Baseline System</strong> with the <strong>Proposed Agentic AI System</strong>. All workflow steps are presented in clean, editable boxes with directional arrows, accompanied by everyday analogies and detailed component explanations.
</div>

<hr style="border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #cbd5e1; margin: 25px 0;">

<!-- ========================================== -->
<!-- PART 1: THE EXISTING SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE   -->
<!-- ========================================== -->

<h2>PART 1: The Existing System Architecture</h2>

<p>In simple terms, the existing system functions like a <strong>one-way conveyor belt</strong>. You place a piece of Python code at the start, it runs through three fixed tools one by one, and it outputs a short explanation along with a static energy estimate.</p>

<table class="flow-table">
    <tr>
        <td class="flow-box">
            User selects or pastes Python code
            <span class="flow-box-sub">(Benchmark or sample code snippet)</span>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr><td class="arrow-down">&darr;</td></tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="flow-box">
            Standard Python Parsing (ast.parse)
            <span class="flow-box-sub">(Parses source code into syntactic tree &bull; Crashes on syntax typos)</span>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr><td class="arrow-down">&darr;</td></tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="flow-box">
            Extract AST Structural Features (ast_extractor.py)
            <span class="flow-box-sub">(Extracts function names, arguments, loops, conditions, and class definitions)</span>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr><td class="arrow-down">&darr;</td></tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="flow-box">
            Extract Program Dependence Graph (pdg_extractor.py)
            <span class="flow-box-sub">(Captures Control Dependencies and Data Flow Edges in plain text)</span>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr><td class="arrow-down">&darr;</td></tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="flow-box">
            Measure Static Green Metrics (green_computing.py)
            <span class="flow-box-sub">(Counts loops, nesting depths, recursion &bull; Computes baseline Eco Score)</span>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr><td class="arrow-down">&darr;</td></tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="flow-box">
            Single-Shot LLM API Call (model_pipeline.py)
            <span class="flow-box-sub">(Assembles prompt with code + PDG text &bull; Calls Groq / HuggingFace model)</span>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr><td class="arrow-down">&darr;</td></tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="flow-box">
            Generate Single Summary Output
            <span class="flow-box-sub">(Produces a single 1-2 sentence English summary &bull; Rule-based fallback if offline)</span>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr><td class="arrow-down">&darr;</td></tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="flow-box">
            Display Results on Streamlit GUI (gui_app.py)
            <span class="flow-box-sub">(Displays text summary, static Graphviz PDG image, and baseline Eco-score)</span>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3>How the Existing Components Work (In Plain English):</h3>

<ol>
    <li><strong>Input & AST Parsing:</strong> When you provide code, Python's built-in parser reads the grammatical structure to produce an <em>Abstract Syntax Tree</em> (the code's skeleton).
    <br><em>Problem:</em> If the user misses a single colon or quotation mark, the program crashes instantly without trying to understand what the user meant.</li>
    
    <li><strong>PDG Dependency Extraction:</strong> It scans the code to see which lines depend on other lines (e.g., Line 5 needs the variable calculated at Line 2). It writes these dependencies out as plain text sentences.</li>
    
    <li><strong>Single-Shot LLM Call:</strong> It packs the code and dependencies into one big prompt and sends it to an open-source model (like Llama 3.1 or Qwen 2.5). The model responds once with a 1-to-2 sentence summary.
    <br><em>Problem:</em> The model doesn't check if its answer was accurate, and it only produces one fixed type of summary.</li>
    
    <li><strong>Green Computing Profiler:</strong> It counts loops, nested levels, and file reads. It uses a mathematical formula to assign an Eco Score out of 100.
    <br><em>Problem:</em> It tells you your code is slow or power-wasting, but it cannot fix the code for you.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout-amber">
    <strong>Summary of Existing Bottlenecks:</strong>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Fragile:</strong> Unhandled syntax typos cause hard crashes.</li>
        <li><strong>Passive:</strong> The AI only speaks when spoken to; it does not verify or self-correct.</li>
        <li><strong>Diagnostic only:</strong> Flags inefficient code, but cannot rewrite it into an efficient version.</li>
        <li><strong>Static UI:</strong> The dependency graph is a flat image that users cannot drag, zoom, or interact with.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<hr style="border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #cbd5e1; margin: 30px 0;">

<!-- ========================================== -->
<!-- PART 2: THE PROPOSED AGENTIC ARCHITECTURE -->
<!-- ========================================== -->

<h2>PART 2: The Proposed Agentic System Architecture</h2>

<p>Instead of a rigid one-way script, the proposed architecture introduces an <strong>Autonomous Multi-Agent Team</strong>. Think of it as a team of 5 specialized digital engineers sitting in a room together, collaborating, testing, and improving your code.</p>

<table class="grid-table">
    <thead>
